WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.278
who remind you that doing science is a privilege.

00:00:12.279 --> 00:00:15.748
The biology students in the Prison University Project

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.316
are just such a group of people.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:22.688
The most fascinating subject that we've studied so far

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:26.759
to me is the human cell, and specifically in the human cell

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:33.799
the protein motor, that is this little protein

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.270
figure carrying these huge objects

00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:39.038
in comparison to its size.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.741
One of the huge concepts that I got from this class was

00:00:41.742 --> 00:00:43.509
when we did photosynthesis

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.446
like the consumption of hydrogen in the plant.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:50.883
Light turning light into cell respiration, right?

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:55.588
And seeing how incredibly efficient life is.

00:00:55.589 --> 00:00:59.125
This CRISPR thing, you take DNA,

00:00:59.126 --> 00:01:02.361
you can take out a bad nucleotide

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.764
and put a good one in. That's amazing!

00:01:04.765 --> 00:01:07.033
The students' passion for biology

00:01:07.034 --> 00:01:09.969
and love of knowledge isn't lost on their instructors.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.473
Adam Williamson and Ryan McGorty are two scientists

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:16.609
that taught the PUP Intro Biology course

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.845
during the fall semester of 2014.

00:01:18.846 --> 00:01:21.447
One of my favorite aspects of the course

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:25.885
was having to do with the students having an immense amount

00:01:25.886 --> 00:01:27.854
of curiosity about biological systems.

00:01:27.855 --> 00:01:30.089
Having a place to put that energy in

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.958
and gain intellectual self-confidence.

00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:36.095
It really surprised us how into it they got.

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:41.033
And how much effort they put into coming up with really creative ideas.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:44.203
Adam and Ryan's biology course fulfills

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:45.705
one of the science requirements

00:01:45.706 --> 00:01:47.673
for the Associate of Arts degree program

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:49.775
offered through the Prison University Project.

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:53.312
PUP is one of the few higher education programs

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.680
in California prisons.

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:59.485
It began in 1996 as an alliance between Patten University,

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:01.954
the education department at San Quentin,

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.190
and a professor from UC Davis.

00:02:04.191 --> 00:02:07.393
It started with donated textbooks and no budget,

00:02:07.394 --> 00:02:10.963
but fundraising efforts have helped support and expand the program

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.031
to what it is today.

00:02:12.032 --> 00:02:16.469
The folks who are incarcerated in any country or in any state

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.405
tend to be the ones who are the most marginalized,

00:02:19.406 --> 00:02:23.276
the disenfranchised, and have had the least opportunities

00:02:23.277 --> 00:02:28.281
educationally, academically, professionally, economically.

00:02:28.282 --> 00:02:30.917
Many of the students we have at San Quentin

00:02:30.918 --> 00:02:32.985
had real struggles when they were in school.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:34.687
Often, the schools were low performing,

00:02:34.688 --> 00:02:36.789
but there may have also been a lot of community violence,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.158
domestic violence. Many of our students were homeless.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:40.393
Or were in foster care.

00:02:40.394 --> 00:02:44.764
So their educational experiences were also severely disrupted.

00:02:44.765 --> 00:02:50.970
Well for myself, being that at one time I can consider myself a chemist,

00:02:50.971 --> 00:02:53.272
I was a street chemist.

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.309
It led me to prison with a life sentence.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:01.247
And when I came here, the only education that I probably had

00:03:01.248 --> 00:03:03.449
at the time was that street education.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.887
And I realized that in order to change who I was

00:03:07.888 --> 00:03:13.726
and no longer lead that particularly confusing life,

00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:15.661
that I needed to educate myself.

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:18.030
What motivates me is my ignorance.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:24.270
I couldn't write an essay, my education was very limited, you know?

00:03:24.271 --> 00:03:26.472
So when I enrolled into that program,

00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:29.275
I do have the ability to learn,

00:03:29.276 --> 00:03:34.280
I can go to college, and it's like, college never even crossed my heart.

00:03:34.281 --> 00:03:38.684
So when they're first in the classroom at San Quentin

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:39.785
there's a lot of work to do.

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:43.389
Not only dealing with academic skill deficits

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.391
but also helping them to get comfortable

00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:48.661
in school, and rediscover an identity as a student.

00:03:48.662 --> 00:03:51.998
And to really start to understand their own academic potential.

00:03:51.999 --> 00:03:55.268
So both Adam and I volunteered to teach

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:58.371
a biology class with lab for the fall semester.

00:03:58.372 --> 00:04:02.675
The class met three times a week, for two or three hours.

00:04:02.676 --> 00:04:05.912
So we're seven hours a week for a 15-week semester

00:04:05.913 --> 00:04:09.048
and one cannot just lecture for that period of time.

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:13.152
So that motivated us to have a lab or an activity

00:04:13.153 --> 00:04:14.954
with pretty much every class period.

00:04:14.955 --> 00:04:17.790
In some cases, for topics one wouldn't expect

00:04:17.791 --> 00:04:20.159
an activity to necessarily be attached.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.796
So one of the more memorable labs that we ran

00:04:23.797 --> 00:04:25.998
was a sea urchin fertilization lab.

00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:30.303
The students actually got to see the living sperm and the egg

00:04:30.304 --> 00:04:32.238
under the microscope, and they were amazed.

00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:35.308
For many of them, they hadn't used a microscope before

00:04:35.309 --> 00:04:40.379
let alone seen a living cell, let alone seen the act of fertilization.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.882
So they were amazed, and then the next day,

00:04:42.883 --> 00:04:45.885
that Monday night following,

00:04:45.886 --> 00:04:49.655
they actually got to see the first stages of fertilization.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:52.825
So I like learning about the cell, you know what I mean?

00:04:52.826 --> 00:04:54.894
Especially what we did yesterday,

00:04:54.895 --> 00:04:57.597
the hands-on project, with the sea urchin.

00:04:57.598 --> 00:04:59.332
Mixing the sperm and the egg together,

00:04:59.333 --> 00:05:00.766
and just watching it develop.

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.568
Yeah, I do enjoy biology.

00:05:02.569 --> 00:05:03.870
I don't know where it will take me.

00:05:03.871 --> 00:05:09.709
But every time I'm here, particularly with these new microscopes

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.313
they give us. I like to get more involved

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:16.816
and I hope I can get involved to the point where

00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:18.918
I can find a way to do research

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:22.388
and find a cure for some disease out there.

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:25.558
I'm considering becoming a drug and alcohol counselor.

00:05:25.559 --> 00:05:28.494
So I think that biology would be extremely important

00:05:28.495 --> 00:05:32.064
in learning the biological makeup of a person,

00:05:32.065 --> 00:05:36.002
if they're predisposed to addiction or not.

00:05:36.003 --> 00:05:38.838
Along with the environmental effects

00:05:38.839 --> 00:05:42.575
and between the two, one would have some

00:05:42.576 --> 00:05:45.978
information on how to arm themselves against that.

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:48.147
I think most students came into the class

00:05:48.148 --> 00:05:50.216
with a pretty strong interest in biology.

00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:53.886
This wasn't a forced class, it was an elective,

00:05:53.887 --> 00:05:56.689
so the students signed up to take this biology class

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.659
and it certainly furthered their interest in biology.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.395
And by the end of the class, the students were asking us,

00:06:02.396 --> 00:06:04.530
"what's next? What's the next science

00:06:04.531 --> 00:06:06.065
or biology class you guys are gonna teach?"

00:06:06.066 --> 00:06:09.202
Or "what other material do you have for me?

00:06:09.203 --> 00:06:11.571
So I can continue to learn biology on my own."

00:06:11.572 --> 00:06:13.639
And a lot of the students didn't want to give back

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.541
their textbooks at the end of the semester.

00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:17.777
They wanted to hold onto them and continue reading them.

00:06:17.778 --> 00:06:22.215
Getting inmates engaged in their education

00:06:22.216 --> 00:06:24.550
is one promising way to reduce the chances

00:06:24.551 --> 00:06:26.853
that released inmates will end up back in prison.

00:06:26.854 --> 00:06:30.723
PUP is one model that empowers inmates intellectually,

00:06:30.724 --> 00:06:33.459
teaches them important work and life skills,

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.528
and builds their self-confidence.

00:06:35.529 --> 00:06:38.264
All of which prepares them for a better transition

00:06:38.265 --> 00:06:40.399
back into society once they are released.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.637
Another thing that continues to keep me motivated

00:06:44.638 --> 00:06:49.742
coming to the PUP class, is the fact that people leave

00:06:49.743 --> 00:06:54.247
parole here, and we get word back on how they're doing.

00:06:54.248 --> 00:06:57.049
Every story that we've heard is that all these guys

00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:59.285
are going to a state university or has a job working

00:06:59.286 --> 00:07:01.354
or he's doing this that or the other.

00:07:01.355 --> 00:07:03.890
PUP has a terrific track record

00:07:03.891 --> 00:07:07.360
for people who successfully complete the program

00:07:07.361 --> 00:07:08.160
to go out and do well.

00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:09.962
And I want that to be my story.

00:07:09.963 --> 00:07:13.232
I don't want to leave prison the same way that I came in.

00:07:13.233 --> 00:07:18.437
So PUP is one of the ways that I've let myself move forward

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:20.306
and it's been a great experience for me.

00:07:20.307 --> 00:07:22.975
So as I spoke about earlier, one of the things

00:07:22.976 --> 00:07:27.547
that I learned from this class is how intimately connected I am

00:07:27.548 --> 00:07:32.385
throughout. And it helps me connect to who I really am.

00:07:32.386 --> 00:07:35.955
To be able to show the passion and love to everyone.

00:07:35.956 --> 00:07:38.891
To use the knowledge I gain from classes like this

00:07:38.892 --> 00:07:43.863
to help the person next to me on their journey in life.

00:07:43.864 --> 00:07:47.800
So it's like that saying of "life only matters

00:07:47.801 --> 00:07:49.902
because of the effects you have on their lives,"

00:07:49.903 --> 00:07:53.739
I don't want the effects that I've had in the past

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.843
to be the effects that I have on the future.

00:07:56.844 --> 00:07:59.679
So in such cases, what motivates me,

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.548
is to get a better understanding of my life.

00:08:02.549 --> 00:08:06.686
It's been life changing, and it has changed my life

00:08:06.687 --> 00:08:09.488
tremendously. I don't know if you can have any idea

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:13.226
the impact you guys have on all of us.

00:08:13.227 --> 00:08:17.330
Because I see people, and I see you guys like leaders

00:08:17.331 --> 00:08:18.831
because you are also an example to us

00:08:18.832 --> 00:08:21.200
you guys do this from your heart

00:08:21.201 --> 00:08:23.769
and I see you guys doing that

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:27.206
and it motivates me to be, in my community, a leader.

00:08:27.207 --> 00:08:31.477
To also practice that, and do things without expecting anything in return.

00:08:31.478 --> 00:08:34.881
And betters our community, and it betters us

00:08:34.882 --> 00:08:38.118
And you make me feel like I can lead, and I appreciate that.

